// A call qualified by a LOCAL interface the builder cannot represent.
//
// `Chooser` is generic, so `implCall`'s ifaceNamed arm finds it, sees it is not
// lowerable, and — because it is not a mirror — declines rather than reporting,
// on the recorded ground that "a non-lowerable LOCAL interface already has its
// own route through the forms below". That route ends at native.go's
// qualifiedCall, which named `qualified call` for the SITE while the interface's
// own `generic interface` refusal was already recorded at the DECLARATION. One
// gap, two names, and the second one was 11 of the corpus key's 84 occurrences.
//
// This program is REFUSED and never runs on the VM; there is no VM output to
// compare. What it pins is the blocker SET, which is the only thing the change
// moves.
//
// The `Display.to_string(1)` beside it is the control, and it is the reason the
// decline lives at the BOTTOM of the chain rather than at the ifaceNamed arm: a
// module may declare its own unrepresentable interface and still make an
// ordinary stdlib-qualified call, and declining early would take that away.
interface Chooser<T> {
    fn left(p: self): T
    fn right(p: self): T
}

struct Pair<E> {
    a: E
    b: E
}

impl Chooser for Pair<E> {
    fn left(p: Pair<E>): E {
        p.a
    }

    fn right(p: Pair<E>): E {
        p.b
    }
}

fn main() {
    _ = Chooser.left(Pair{a: 10, b: 20})
    _ = Display.to_string(1)
}
